title: 'AI Screening Interviews: Replace 30 Phone Screens With One-Page Reports'
publishedAt: 2026-10-21
type: companies
---
**Quick answer:** GetHired's AI screening interview is a short, structured voice interview that a company adds to a job post. Applicants above your bar are invited after they apply, take the interview in their browser, and you get a one-page report scoring each competency you chose, with exact quotes from the interview as evidence, plus concerns and follow-up questions for your own interview. A person on your team makes every decision.

The first phone screen is the most expensive part of early-stage hiring. Thirty calls of twenty minutes each is a full working day, and most of them end with "nice person, not quite the fit". Resumes don't tell you how someone explains their work, and take-home tests put off good candidates.

A screening interview sits in between: a consistent fifteen minutes with every promising applicant, without the calendar ping-pong.

## How it works

### 1. You set it up on the job

Open the job post and go to its **AI interview** section. Choose:

- **Who gets invited.** Applicants whose [AI fit score](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/ai-applicant-screening-how-to-sort-200-applicants-by-fit-without-reading-them-all) is at or above your threshold are invited automatically, or you can invite people by hand.
- **Must-ask questions.** The questions every candidate should answer, such as "Walk me through a production incident you handled."
- **Competencies.** Up to five things to score, such as system design, debugging, communication or ownership.
- **Length and deadline.** How long the interview runs and how many days candidates have to take it.

You can save sets of questions to reuse across similar roles.

### 2. The candidate applies first

The application is saved before any interview happens, so nobody loses their application because of a bad connection. Invited applicants see the invite right after applying ("Start now" or "Do it later"), by email and on their GetHired dashboard. They get reminders before the deadline.

### 3. A structured voice interview

The interviewer covers your must-ask questions, follows up on vague answers and keeps to time. Every candidate for the job gets the same structure, which makes the reports comparable.

Each candidate gets **one attempt**. If an attempt fails for a technical reason, such as a dropped connection, they get one retry.

### 4. You read a one-page report

After the interview, you get a company-side report:

- a **score for each competency**, with the overall score computed from them;
- **evidence**: short quotes from what the candidate actually said. Quotes that don't appear word for word in the transcript are removed before you see them;
- **concerns** worth probing;
- **follow-up questions** for your own interview.

There's deliberately no "hire" or "reject" recommendation. The report tells you what the candidate said and how it maps to your competencies, and you decide what happens next.

## Where it fits in your pipeline

The interview status appears in the applications list, on the [pipeline board](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/a-lightweight-ats-for-startups-pipeline-stages-boards-notes-and-email-templates) and on each application's page.

- **Bulk invite** applicants from the list, up to 50 at a time.
- **Extend, cancel or reopen** an interview from the application page. Reopening a cancelled or expired interview re-sends the invite.
- **Moving someone to Rejected or Hired** cancels their open interview automatically.

You can also ask the [recruiter agent](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/meet-the-gethired-recruiter-agent-hire-tech-talent-from-one-chat) to "invite every applicant above 75 to the AI interview". It shows you the list first.

## Trustworthy by design

The difference between a practice tool and a screening step is whether you can rely on the result. A few rules make that possible:

- **The transcript is recorded on our servers**, by the voice service, not uploaded by the candidate's browser.
- **One attempt** plus one technical retry, so the result isn't the best of ten tries.
- **Must-ask questions stay private.** Applicants can't see them before the interview.
- **Evidence is verified** against the transcript.
- **Fairness rules apply.** The analysis scores job-related competencies only, and your question text is checked so it doesn't ask about protected characteristics. See [fair AI hiring](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/fair-ai-hiring-how-gethired-keeps-bias-out-of-candidate-screening).
- **Old transcripts are deleted** on a retention schedule.

## Pricing: pay only for completed interviews

Interviews use interview minutes from your plan. Minutes are reserved when you invite someone, and charged only when the candidate completes the interview. If an invite expires, is cancelled, or the applicant is moved to Rejected or Hired before taking it, the minutes are released. Check the current allowances on the [pricing page](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/pricing/companies).

## Tips for a good screening interview

1. **Pick competencies the role really needs.** Three sharp competencies beat five vague ones.
2. **Write must-ask questions about past work.** "Tell me about a time you…" gets better evidence than hypotheticals.
3. **Set the fit threshold around 75.** That invites strong matches without flooding candidates who are clearly off-target.
4. **Use the follow-up questions.** They're the fastest way to prepare for the human interview.
5. **Tell candidates what to expect** in the job description. A sentence like "Shortlisted applicants take a 15-minute AI interview" sets the right expectation.

Applicants can read our [guide to taking an AI screening interview](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/the-new-gethired-for-companies-what-it-means-for-job-seekers).

